1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,143
Imagine a bright white light, brighter than the sun, over a city of tens of thousands.

2
00:00:06,143 --> 00:00:08,777
Seconds later, a wall of heat and wind.

3
00:00:08,777 --> 00:00:10,466
Nothing is left standing.

4
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:16,302
That is the story told about this hill in Jordan, called Tall el-Hammam.

5
00:00:16,302 --> 00:00:20,106
A comet blast, three and a half thousand years ago.

6
00:00:20,106 --> 00:00:22,641
A real Bronze Age city, wiped out.

7
00:00:22,641 --> 00:00:24,133
Possibly even Sodom.

8
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:30,673
And in two thousand twenty-one, a respected journal published a paper that said it was proven.

9
00:00:30,673 --> 00:00:35,066
Then, in two thousand twenty-five, that same journal pulled it back.

10
00:00:35,533 --> 00:00:38,492
So tonight we sit at the retraction desk.

11
00:00:38,492 --> 00:00:45,133
We take the claim apart line by line, and we mark every line: evidence, theory, or debunked.

12
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,213
First, the site itself.

13
00:00:47,213 --> 00:00:50,931
Because the real story is stranger than the headline.

14
00:00:50,931 --> 00:00:51,633
Case open.

15
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:56,959
Chapter one.

16
00:00:56,959 --> 00:00:57,604
The site.

17
00:00:57,604 --> 00:01:05,700
Tall el-Hammam lies in the Jordan Valley, east of the river, about thirteen kilometres northeast of the Dead Sea.

18
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:07,384
The mound is huge.

19
00:01:07,384 --> 00:01:15,433
The fortified city covered about twenty-six hectares, and the upper part of the hill stands roughly thirty metres high.

20
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:18,821
People lived here for thousands of years.

21
00:01:18,821 --> 00:01:26,233
From the Chalcolithic, about four thousand BC, through the Iron Age, and into Roman and Byzantine times.

22
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:33,493
But the city was biggest during the Middle Bronze Age, around two thousand to sixteen hundred BC.

23
00:01:33,493 --> 00:01:38,466
At that time it was one of the largest settlements in the whole region.

24
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:41,441
The name means Hill of the Hot Baths.

25
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:47,000
In twenty eleven, diggers found a Byzantine bath, which fits the name beautifully.

26
00:01:47,466 --> 00:01:52,847
The American scholar Nelson Glueck surveyed the valley in nineteen forty-one.

27
00:01:52,847 --> 00:01:56,900
He tied this area to a biblical place called Abel Shittim.

28
00:01:57,366 --> 00:02:00,892
Real excavation began in the nineteen seventies.

29
00:02:00,892 --> 00:02:08,533
Then from two thousand five to two thousand sixteen, a team led by Steven Collins dug here every season.

30
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,470
And Collins argued something bold.

31
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:13,431
This was not just any city.

32
00:02:13,431 --> 00:02:17,500
It was Sodom, the city destroyed in the book of Genesis.

33
00:02:17,966 --> 00:02:20,751
Mainstream archaeologists rejected that.

34
00:02:20,751 --> 00:02:23,257
So did many other biblical scholars.

35
00:02:23,257 --> 00:02:28,199
But a theory needs a destroyed city, so let us look at the destruction.

36
00:02:28,666 --> 00:02:29,452
Chapter two.

37
00:02:29,452 --> 00:02:30,893
The destruction layer.

38
00:02:30,893 --> 00:02:38,033
In the trenches, the diggers found a dark band of burnt material, with broken pottery and collapsed mudbrick.

39
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:45,033
Some of the pottery looked glassy on the outside, as if it had been heated far beyond a normal fire.

40
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,391
Some walls had collapsed in a single direction.

41
00:02:48,391 --> 00:02:53,066
And, the team said, the upper part of the settlement had been stripped away.

42
00:02:53,533 --> 00:02:58,125
After that, the area seems to have been abandoned for hundreds of years.

43
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:01,633
A real gap in the story, which happens to matter later.

44
00:03:02,099 --> 00:03:08,498
In two thousand twenty-one, the team published their explanation in Scientific Reports.

45
00:03:08,498 --> 00:03:13,500
The title spoke of a Bronze Age city destroyed by a cosmic airburst.

46
00:03:13,966 --> 00:03:16,933
The evidence they listed was microscopic.

47
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:19,611
Tiny melted grains, called spherules.

48
00:03:19,611 --> 00:03:20,696
Shocked quartz.

49
00:03:20,696 --> 00:03:24,966
Zircon crystals that looked heated to extreme temperatures.

50
00:03:25,433 --> 00:03:35,133
Their picture: a small comet fragment explodes in the air, like Tunguska, nineteen oh eight, and the blast and heat destroy everything below.

51
00:03:35,599 --> 00:03:39,781
They claimed temperatures above two thousand degrees Celsius.

52
00:03:39,781 --> 00:03:44,099
Hot enough to melt rock, and to turn ordinary pottery to glass.

53
00:03:44,566 --> 00:03:46,192
It is a gripping story.

54
00:03:46,192 --> 00:03:51,494
A one-off, instant, city-killing blast, ending in a salt-crusted wasteland.

55
00:03:51,494 --> 00:03:52,766
So who checked it?

56
00:03:53,233 --> 00:03:54,142
Chapter three.

57
00:03:54,142 --> 00:03:54,857
The review.

58
00:03:54,857 --> 00:03:57,519
Because science is not what a paper says.

59
00:03:57,519 --> 00:03:59,533
It is what survives afterwards.

60
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,610
Start with who wrote it.

61
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:10,599
A large share of the authors belonged to the Comet Research Group, a body which sponsors the idea that comets repeatedly struck Earth.

62
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:12,990
That does not make them wrong.

63
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:18,633
But it does mean the airburst was a thesis they had been defending long before this dig.

64
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:20,906
Next, the outside experts.

65
00:04:20,906 --> 00:04:28,966
The physicist Mark Boslough, whose own work is cited in the paper, said the paper's reading of his models was wrong.

66
00:04:29,433 --> 00:04:30,894
Then came the images.

67
00:04:30,894 --> 00:04:38,199
The scientific-integrity researcher Elisabeth Bik showed that some figures in the paper had been altered.

68
00:04:38,666 --> 00:04:46,199
In March twenty twenty-two, an independent review concluded that the criteria for an airburst were simply not met.

69
00:04:46,666 --> 00:04:47,986
Questions piled up.

70
00:04:47,986 --> 00:04:54,099
Were the melted grains from the destruction layer, or just from ordinary fires and dirt?

71
00:04:54,099 --> 00:04:58,266
Were the temperatures real, or estimated from an assumption?

72
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:06,733
In February twenty twenty-three, the journal added an editor's note, warning readers that the conclusions were under question.

73
00:05:07,199 --> 00:05:14,177
And on the twenty-fourth of April, twenty twenty-five, Scientific Reports retracted the paper.

74
00:05:14,177 --> 00:05:19,300
Reason: concerns about methodology, analysis and data interpretation.

75
00:05:19,766 --> 00:05:20,587
Chapter four.

76
00:05:20,587 --> 00:05:22,356
What the ground really says.

77
00:05:22,356 --> 00:05:25,008
Strip away the airburst, and what is left?

78
00:05:25,008 --> 00:05:25,766
Quite a lot.

79
00:05:26,233 --> 00:05:32,260
Tall el-Hammam was a major walled city, with huge ramparts and a large population.

80
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:34,833
That is fact, dug out of the earth.

81
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:39,070
Something did end that city, or at least reduced it.

82
00:05:39,070 --> 00:05:40,665
Burnt layers are real.

83
00:05:40,665 --> 00:05:45,233
Many ancient cities were burned by war, earthquake or accident.

84
00:05:45,699 --> 00:05:47,828
Mudbrick cities burn easily.

85
00:05:47,828 --> 00:05:49,957
Roofs held timber and reeds.

86
00:05:49,957 --> 00:05:54,899
A siege, or a single bad night, can fill a trench with black ash.

87
00:05:55,366 --> 00:05:59,924
The valley also sits on the Dead Sea Transform, a major fault line.

88
00:05:59,924 --> 00:06:03,733
Earthquakes here are not a theory, they are a certainty.

89
00:06:04,199 --> 00:06:10,722
Tilted walls and collapsed bricks are what you would expect after an earthquake, with fire after it.

90
00:06:10,722 --> 00:06:11,766
No comet needed.

91
00:06:12,233 --> 00:06:13,622
And the abandonment?

92
00:06:13,622 --> 00:06:16,052
Many sites in the valley show gaps.

93
00:06:16,052 --> 00:06:20,566
Drought, shifting trade routes and conquest can all empty a city.

94
00:06:21,033 --> 00:06:23,850
That is the humbling part of archaeology.

95
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:26,186
A gap tells us something happened.

96
00:06:26,186 --> 00:06:27,766
It never tells us what.

97
00:06:28,233 --> 00:06:31,800
So the ground supports a great city and a burnt end.

98
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:35,366
It does not, by itself, support a bomb from the sky.

99
00:06:35,833 --> 00:06:36,747
Chapter five.

100
00:06:36,747 --> 00:06:38,013
The Sodom problem.

101
00:06:38,013 --> 00:06:41,389
The airburst claim was never just about science.

102
00:06:41,389 --> 00:06:42,866
It was about a story.

103
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:50,966
Genesis tells of cities destroyed by fire and brimstone, and the Dead Sea region is where tradition places them.

104
00:06:51,433 --> 00:06:53,984
Plenty of places have been proposed.

105
00:06:53,984 --> 00:06:58,448
Some scholars say the cities lie beneath the southern Dead Sea.

106
00:06:58,448 --> 00:07:00,149
Others say Bab edh-Dhra.

107
00:07:00,149 --> 00:07:01,566
Collins says Hammam.

108
00:07:02,033 --> 00:07:03,824
The dates are one problem.

109
00:07:03,824 --> 00:07:12,433
Collins matched the destruction to the Middle Bronze Age, but the Genesis story is told, in tradition, about a different era.

110
00:07:12,899 --> 00:07:14,579
The geography is another.

111
00:07:14,579 --> 00:07:21,833
Texts place Sodom near the Dead Sea's plain, with the plain being called a circle, or kikkar, of the Jordan.

112
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:25,614
But nothing found at Hammam names it Sodom.

113
00:07:25,614 --> 00:07:28,158
No inscription, no seal, no text.

114
00:07:28,158 --> 00:07:32,166
The identification is an interpretation of a legend.

115
00:07:32,633 --> 00:07:34,493
There is a modern cost, too.

116
00:07:34,493 --> 00:07:42,133
After the claim went viral, sites like this attract attention, and in the Middle East, attention can bring looters.

117
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:45,778
Faith and archaeology can sit side by side.

118
00:07:45,778 --> 00:07:51,100
But when a dig is led by a conclusion, the data tends to bend toward it.

119
00:07:51,566 --> 00:07:55,207
So can a real catastrophe still hide in the sand?

120
00:07:55,207 --> 00:07:58,699
Let us test the airburst idea on its own terms.

121
00:07:59,166 --> 00:08:00,075
Chapter six.

122
00:08:00,075 --> 00:08:00,984
The physics.

123
00:08:00,984 --> 00:08:02,423
Airbursts are real.

124
00:08:02,423 --> 00:08:08,633
In nineteen oh eight, a blast flattened eighty million trees in Tunguska, Siberia.

125
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:13,740
In twenty thirteen, a smaller one burst over Chelyabinsk, Russia.

126
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:19,166
The shockwave broke thousands of windows and injured over a thousand people.

127
00:08:19,633 --> 00:08:22,194
But notice what those blasts did not do.

128
00:08:22,194 --> 00:08:27,699
They did not melt cities into glass, and they did not stay hot enough to bake pottery.

129
00:08:28,166 --> 00:08:30,748
Heat from an airburst lasts seconds.

130
00:08:30,748 --> 00:08:33,760
Melting thick mudbrick or sand takes time.

131
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:39,066
The numbers in the paper needed a hotter, longer fire than physics allows.

132
00:08:39,533 --> 00:08:45,955
And ordinary fires, with timber roofs and olive oil, can reach surprisingly high heat.

133
00:08:45,955 --> 00:08:49,166
Some glassy slag is common in burnt cities.

134
00:08:49,633 --> 00:08:51,381
Spherules sound dramatic.

135
00:08:51,381 --> 00:08:57,253
But they rain constantly from space, and form from welding, volcanoes and lightning.

136
00:08:57,253 --> 00:08:59,699
Their presence alone proves little.

137
00:09:00,166 --> 00:09:02,146
Even the human story is thin.

138
00:09:02,146 --> 00:09:10,000
The paper described human remains, but critics said the evidence for a blast, rather than a collapse, is not clear.

139
00:09:10,466 --> 00:09:11,225
No crater.

140
00:09:11,225 --> 00:09:12,212
No meteorite.

141
00:09:12,212 --> 00:09:13,881
No clear shocked rock.

142
00:09:13,881 --> 00:09:18,966
An airburst leaves clues, and these clues are missing or contested.

143
00:09:19,433 --> 00:09:20,378
Chapter seven.

144
00:09:20,378 --> 00:09:21,188
The lessons.

145
00:09:21,188 --> 00:09:23,079
What does a retraction mean?

146
00:09:23,079 --> 00:09:25,172
It does not prove the opposite.

147
00:09:25,172 --> 00:09:27,399
It means the claim was not shown.

148
00:09:27,866 --> 00:09:29,387
The dig itself stands.

149
00:09:29,387 --> 00:09:35,333
The pottery, the walls, the ramparts: those are still real data that anyone can study.

150
00:09:35,799 --> 00:09:37,657
And the story is a warning.

151
00:09:37,657 --> 00:09:42,266
When we love a dramatic answer, we quietly lower the bar for proof.

152
00:09:42,733 --> 00:09:44,933
Which brings us to the scorecard.

153
00:09:44,933 --> 00:09:46,933
One at a time, claim by claim.

154
00:09:47,399 --> 00:09:49,500
Retraction desk, closing the file.

155
00:09:49,966 --> 00:09:50,850
The scorecard.

156
00:09:50,850 --> 00:09:52,366
Evidence against theory.

157
00:09:52,833 --> 00:09:56,683
A large Middle Bronze Age city at Hammam: evidence.

158
00:09:56,683 --> 00:09:58,722
A burnt end layer: partial.

159
00:09:58,722 --> 00:10:02,195
Earthquake and fire as explanation: plausible.

160
00:10:02,195 --> 00:10:05,366
Heat above two thousand degrees: doubtful.

161
00:10:05,833 --> 00:10:09,595
The Genesis city of Sodom identified: unproven.

162
00:10:09,595 --> 00:10:12,637
The paper's airburst claim: retracted.

163
00:10:12,637 --> 00:10:16,799
A comet blast as alien disguise: no evidence at all.

164
00:10:17,266 --> 00:10:18,118
The verdict.

165
00:10:18,118 --> 00:10:22,025
A real city, a real mystery, but not a proven airburst.

166
00:10:22,025 --> 00:10:26,500
The most dramatic explanation turned out to be the weakest one.

167
00:10:26,966 --> 00:10:30,042
And the better story is that science worked.

168
00:10:30,042 --> 00:10:34,027
A bold claim was made, tested, challenged, and withdrawn.

169
00:10:34,027 --> 00:10:37,033
That is how the machine is supposed to run.

170
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:46,196
Next case: the Hessdalen lights, a valley in Norway where strange glowing balls have been drifting over the hills for decades.

171
00:10:46,196 --> 00:10:48,266
Plasma, or something stranger?

172
00:10:48,733 --> 00:10:52,733
Do you think a catastrophe from the sky is still possible here?

173
00:10:52,733 --> 00:10:58,066
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
